  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A sonar scan showed a 200-foot disc on the seabed. June 2011. The Gulf of Bothnia, in the northern Baltic, between Sweden and Finland.",
        "visual_query": "baltic sea sonar vessel",
        "visual_fallback": "cold open sea"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The Swedish team hunting wrecks \u2014 Peter Lindberg and Dennis Asberg \u2014 said it had features of seemingly non-natural origin. The tabloids printed crashed saucer.",
        "visual_query": "swedish archipelago cold sea",
        "visual_fallback": "baltic coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They reported right angles. A track running up to it like a runway. And then they brought pieces of it back up. So what was actually down there?",
        "visual_query": "deep sea diver underwater",
        "visual_fallback": "underwater diver"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Rock. Volker Bruchert at Stockholm University identified the samples as granite, gneiss and sandstone \u2014 ordinary stone, dropped here by ice age glaciers.",
        "visual_query": "granite boulder rocky seabed",
        "visual_fallback": "granite rock"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A Finnish geomorphologist read the runway as a natural drumlin. And Woods Hole called the famous sonar image virtually useless: cheap gear, badly calibrated.",
        "visual_query": "glacial rock formation scandinavia",
        "visual_fallback": "glacial boulders"
      }
    ],
